    "['no shutdown', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/3 disable false', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/3 disable false\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Junos, \"no shutdown\" is equivalent to enabling the interface, which is achieved by setting \"disable false\" (or alternatively \"delete interfaces ge-1/0/3 disable\" would remove any shutdown configuration). The command is interface-specific, so I translated it for GigabitEthernet1/0/3 as an example. You would use similar syntax for other interfaces (ge-1/0/1, ge-1/0/2, etc.).', 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/3 disable false'}]]": 1,
    "['switchport trunk allowed vlan [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/3 unit 0 family ethernet-switching vlan members 100', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco command `switchport trunk allowed vlan 100`:\\n\\n##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/3 unit 0 family ethernet-switching vlan members 100\\n##\\n\\nThis Juniper command:\\n1. Configures interface ge-1/0/3 (GigabitEthernet1/0/3)\\n2. Sets it as a trunk interface (implied by the ethernet-switching)\\n3. Allows only VLAN 100 to pass through (equivalent to Cisco's allowed vlan 100)\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, trunk interfaces automatically allow all VLANs by default, so we explicitly specify which VLANs are allowed using the `vlan members` statement.\", 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/3 unit 0 family ethernet-switching vlan members 100'}]]": 1,
    "['no shutdown', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/3 disable false', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/3 disable false\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, `no shutdown` is equivalent to enabling the interface, which is done by setting `disable` to `false`. The interface naming follows Juniper's convention (ge-1/0/3 instead of GigabitEthernet1/0/3). This translation applies to all three interfaces in the original configuration, but since you asked for a single command translation, I've provided it for GE1/0/3 as an example.\", 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/3 disable false'}]]": 1,
    "['switchport trunk allowed vlan add [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 0 family ethernet-switching vlan members [ 10 100 ]', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco command:\\n\\n##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 0 family ethernet-switching vlan members [ 10 100 ]\\n##\\n\\nNote:\\n1. In Juniper, we specify VLAN members under the interface configuration\\n2. The VLANs are listed in square brackets separated by spaces (not commas)\\n3. The interface naming follows Juniper\\'s convention (ge-1/0/1 instead of GigabitEthernet1/0/1)\\n4. This assumes the interface is already configured as a trunk (equivalent to \"switchport mode trunk\")', 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 0 family ethernet-switching vlan members [ 10 100 ]'}]]": 1,
    "['no shutdown', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/3 disable false', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/3 disable false\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, the equivalent of Cisco\\'s \"no shutdown\" is \"disable false\" (or alternatively \"delete disable\" if the interface was previously shut down). The interface naming convention also changes from Cisco\\'s \"GigabitEthernet1/0/3\" to Juniper\\'s \"ge-1/0/3\". \\n\\nFor completeness, the translation applies to all three interfaces in your example (ge-1/0/1, ge-1/0/2, ge-1/0/3), but since you asked specifically about \"no shutdown\", I provided just one example.', 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/3 disable false'}]]": 1,
    "['switchport trunk allowed vlan add [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/2 unit 0 family ethernet-switching vlan members [20 100]', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translated Juniper equivalent command:\\n\\n##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/2 unit 0 family ethernet-switching vlan members [20 100]\\n##\\n\\nOr alternatively, if you need to add these VLANs to an existing trunk configuration:\\n\\n##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/2 unit 0 family ethernet-switching vlan members 20\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/2 unit 0 family ethernet-switching vlan members 100\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, there\\'s no direct \"add\" operation like Cisco - you either set the complete list or add members individually. The first example shows the complete VLAN membership setting, while the second shows how you would add VLANs individually to an existing configuration.', 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/2 unit 0 family ethernet-switching vlan members [20 100]'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/2 unit 0 family ethernet-switching vlan members 20', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translated Juniper equivalent command:\\n\\n##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/2 unit 0 family ethernet-switching vlan members [20 100]\\n##\\n\\nOr alternatively, if you need to add these VLANs to an existing trunk configuration:\\n\\n##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/2 unit 0 family ethernet-switching vlan members 20\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/2 unit 0 family ethernet-switching vlan members 100\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, there\\'s no direct \"add\" operation like Cisco - you either set the complete list or add members individually. The first example shows the complete VLAN membership setting, while the second shows how you would add VLANs individually to an existing configuration.', 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/2 unit 0 family ethernet-switching vlan members 20'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/2 unit 0 family ethernet-switching vlan members 100', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translated Juniper equivalent command:\\n\\n##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/2 unit 0 family ethernet-switching vlan members [20 100]\\n##\\n\\nOr alternatively, if you need to add these VLANs to an existing trunk configuration:\\n\\n##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/2 unit 0 family ethernet-switching vlan members 20\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/2 unit 0 family ethernet-switching vlan members 100\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Juniper, there\\'s no direct \"add\" operation like Cisco - you either set the complete list or add members individually. The first example shows the complete VLAN membership setting, while the second shows how you would add VLANs individually to an existing configuration.', 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/2 unit 0 family ethernet-switching vlan members 100'}]]": 1
